WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.506
This briefing from the Federal Modernization
Workbook takes on one investment

3
00:00:05.506 --> 00:00:09.612
question: what to do about the network
behind an agency's field offices.

4
00:00:09.612 --> 00:00:12.829
A familiar proposal asks
for about fifty million

5
00:00:12.829 --> 00:00:16.201
dollars to refresh a content
server in every office.

6
00:00:16.201 --> 00:00:20.134
I'll make the case that the problem
is the circuit, not the content,

7
00:00:20.134 --> 00:00:23.325
and that fixing the circuit
costs less and does more.

8
00:00:23.325 --> 00:00:27.173
You'll see the honest limits,
the costs and their assumptions,

9
00:00:27.173 --> 00:00:31.070
the security conditions, and
the one decision to ask for.

10
00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.207
Here's why this matters.

11
00:00:33.207 --> 00:00:36.921
The worked case is an estate
many agencies will recognize:

12
00:00:36.921 --> 00:00:42.103
about fifteen hundred field offices,
about seventy thousand Windows endpoints,

13
00:00:42.103 --> 00:00:44.888
and a content server in
nearly every office,

14
00:00:44.888 --> 00:00:48.446
because the circuit was once
too slow for anything else.

15
00:00:48.446 --> 00:00:51.441
Microsoft's published limits
say that estate needs

16
00:00:51.441 --> 00:00:54.645
about twenty-four of those
servers, not fifteen hundred.

17
00:00:54.645 --> 00:00:59.170
The other fourteen hundred and seventy-six
compensate for slow branch circuits.

18
00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:03.495
And the money would land on a tier the
device strategy is already shrinking.

19
00:01:03.495 --> 00:01:06.250
So what would fixing the
circuit actually buy?

20
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.112
Let's be honest about the limits first.

21
00:01:09.112 --> 00:01:12.518
Part of every delay is the time
light takes through fiber,

22
00:01:12.518 --> 00:01:16.576
about five microseconds per kilometer,
and no product changes that.

23
00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:21.407
What SD-WAN does is close the gap above
that floor: better paths, a tighter tail

24
00:01:21.407 --> 00:01:22.864
through fast failover,

25
00:01:22.864 --> 00:01:26.775
and bulk downloads kept away from
traffic that has to be quick.

26
00:01:26.775 --> 00:01:30.556
Each office gets two paths,
an internet circuit sized

27
00:01:30.556 --> 00:01:34.337
to its seats and a 5G link
for continuity, not capacity.

28
00:01:34.337 --> 00:01:36.662
It's a better road, not a shorter one.

29
00:01:36.662 --> 00:01:38.740
Here's what the target looks like.

30
00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:41.440
The target fits in one picture.

31
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.877
Two data centers hold the
full content library and

32
00:01:44.877 --> 00:01:48.315
feed ten regional hubs,
all in existing facilities.

33
00:01:48.315 --> 00:01:51.397
Each hub carries two
servers, twenty-four in all,

34
00:01:51.397 --> 00:01:56.928
enough for ninety-six thousand clients, so
about seventy-three percent utilization.

35
00:01:56.928 --> 00:02:01.614
Offices reach the hubs over the SD-WAN
overlay with no server in the closet,

36
00:02:01.614 --> 00:02:04.303
and peer caching keeps most content local.

37
00:02:04.303 --> 00:02:09.747
One limit is settled: Microsoft's informed
routing feature isn't available in GCC,

38
00:02:09.747 --> 00:02:15.028
so the design uses direct breakout and
application-aware routing from day one.

39
00:02:15.028 --> 00:02:16.800
Now, what does it cost?

40
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.573
On the book's planning assumptions,

41
00:02:19.573 --> 00:02:23.201
the first year needs just under
ten million dollars of capital:

42
00:02:23.201 --> 00:02:27.247
about five million for branch equipment,
about one million at the hubs,

43
00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:31.712
and nearly four million for the control
plane, installation and engineering.

44
00:02:31.712 --> 00:02:33.745
At steady state, the new circuits,

45
00:02:33.745 --> 00:02:39.012
subscriptions and security licensing run
about fifteen point seven million a year,

46
00:02:39.012 --> 00:02:43.000
while retiring MPLS removes about
twenty-one point six million.

47
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.812
That's roughly five point
nine million a year back.

48
00:02:45.812 --> 00:02:49.220
Every figure is an estimate to
replace with your own data,

49
00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.620
and one matters more than the rest.

50
00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:57.545
Savings arrive as offices move at their
MPLS renewal dates, not on day one.

51
00:02:57.545 --> 00:03:01.446
In the base case the program turns
cumulatively positive in year

52
00:03:01.446 --> 00:03:05.420
four and is about seven point seven
million ahead after five years.

53
00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:08.002
The refresh spends fifty million up front,

54
00:03:08.002 --> 00:03:12.207
so the five-year difference is about
fifty-seven point seven million.

55
00:03:12.207 --> 00:03:15.532
But all of that turns on what
MPLS costs per office today.

56
00:03:15.532 --> 00:03:18.932
Breakeven is eight hundred
seventy-three dollars a month.

57
00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:21.508
Above it, the program pays for itself.

58
00:03:21.508 --> 00:03:25.883
Below it, it's still the better
architecture, argued on capability.

59
00:03:25.883 --> 00:03:27.520
Then there's security.

60
00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:32.296
Breaking out at the branch means
operating under the TIC 3.0

61
00:03:32.296 --> 00:03:35.382
branch office use case, and
that changes what the edge is.

62
00:03:35.382 --> 00:03:38.730
Every path has to land on
equally strong enforcement,

63
00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:42.382
so the steering policy itself
becomes a security control.

64
00:03:42.382 --> 00:03:46.397
Accreditation, not procurement, is
the critical path, which is why

65
00:03:46.397 --> 00:03:51.082
starting at twelve hubs instead of
fifteen hundred offices matters so much.

66
00:03:51.082 --> 00:03:56.483
No office's MPLS circuit retires until
its replacement telemetry is confirmed.

67
00:03:56.483 --> 00:03:59.465
And the controller is
estate-wide attack surface,

68
00:03:59.465 --> 00:04:03.320
so it's patched, logged and locked
down before it carries traffic.

69
00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:05.480
That sets the roadmap's order.

70
00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:07.580
There are four phases.

71
00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:12.162
Phase zero, about six months and
about one point eight million dollars,

72
00:04:12.162 --> 00:04:17.455
does the design, the accreditation package,
the control plane and a ten-site pilot.

73
00:04:17.455 --> 00:04:21.718
Phase one builds the twelve hubs
and the consolidated content tier.

74
00:04:21.718 --> 00:04:25.511
Phase two moves offices in
waves at their MPLS renewals,

75
00:04:25.511 --> 00:04:27.655
so each wave helps fund the next.

76
00:04:27.655 --> 00:04:33.368
Phase three adds Microsoft 365 path
optimization and caching at the hubs.

77
00:04:33.368 --> 00:04:38.599
Meanwhile, a federal directive requires
end-of-support edge devices to be replaced

78
00:04:38.599 --> 00:04:39.887
by about August 2027,

79
00:04:39.887 --> 00:04:43.905
so write the target's requirements
into those replacements now.

80
00:04:43.905 --> 00:04:46.080
And the finish line isn't a date.

81
00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:50.905
The twenty-four-server tier is an
interim state, not a new floor.

82
00:04:50.905 --> 00:04:53.645
The last one retires
when five gates close:

83
00:04:53.645 --> 00:04:59.125
application and update content have moved
to Intune, imaging has moved to Autopilot,

84
00:04:59.125 --> 00:05:01.474
no cache still runs on that hardware,

85
00:05:01.474 --> 00:05:05.780
and no compliance evidence still
comes from the old site database.

86
00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:10.505
An estate that closes them in month
thirty retires the tier in month thirty.

87
00:05:10.505 --> 00:05:12.846
One that hasn't by month sixty keeps it,

88
00:05:12.846 --> 00:05:16.018
rather than forcing a
cutover against an open gate.

89
00:05:16.018 --> 00:05:18.040
Next, what could go wrong.

90
00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:20.502
Three risks are rated high.

91
00:05:20.502 --> 00:05:24.308
Accreditation could slip; a
twelve-month slip cuts the five-year

92
00:05:24.308 --> 00:05:28.114
position to about one point eight
million, but the case survives.

93
00:05:28.114 --> 00:05:30.448
MPLS might cost less than assumed,

94
00:05:30.448 --> 00:05:34.115
which moves the argument
from savings to capability.

95
00:05:34.115 --> 00:05:38.590
And telemetry could lag cutover, which
is why it gates every retirement.

96
00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:44.202
Behind those sit ten assumptions; check
MPLS cost and the endpoint count first.

97
00:05:44.202 --> 00:05:48.975
The book is plain about it: the architecture
holds across the plausible range,

98
00:05:48.975 --> 00:05:53.152
but the financial case holds only
once the MPLS number is confirmed.

99
00:05:53.152 --> 00:05:54.850
So here's the ask.

100
00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:57.225
The ask has three parts.

101
00:05:57.225 --> 00:06:01.290
Defer the fifty-million-dollar
refresh pending a network assessment,

102
00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:05.775
and have the sponsor say whether that
figure is acquisition or five-year cost.

103
00:06:05.775 --> 00:06:09.137
Validate the assumptions,
starting with MPLS.

104
00:06:09.137 --> 00:06:13.625
And fund Phase zero, about one
point eight million over six months,

105
00:06:13.625 --> 00:06:16.367
with a named owner and a clear first gate:

106
00:06:16.367 --> 00:06:21.601
authorization for regional breakout, a
stable pilot, and real per-site costs.

107
00:06:21.601 --> 00:06:26.838
Before scaling out, prove it with a
paired-branch study: two comparable offices,

108
00:06:26.838 --> 00:06:30.625
measured for thirty days, a
change at one, thirty more days.

109
00:06:30.625 --> 00:06:33.340
That difference is your
business-case number.

110
00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:38.412
To go deeper, Book 09, Chapter
11 has the full worked case,

111
00:06:38.412 --> 00:06:41.390
with every assumption and its source.

112
00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:46.752
Chapter 05 covers what SD-WAN can and
can't do, and how to secure the controller.

113
00:06:46.752 --> 00:06:52.350
Chapter 03 sets out the TIC 3.0
requirements, Chapter 10 the long interim and

114
00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:57.103
the refresh fork, and Chapter 13 the
sequencing and the paired-branch study.

115
00:06:57.103 --> 00:07:01.752
The Book 09 executive briefing
covers the wider network decisions.

116
00:07:01.752 --> 00:07:04.839
Every figure here is a planning
estimate from the book.

117
00:07:04.839 --> 00:07:05.670
Thank you.
